{"course": {"schema_version": "1.0", "course_id": "mitocw-3-a08-fall-2005", "institution": "Massachusetts Institute of Technology", "platform": "MIT OpenCourseWare", "course_codes": ["3.A08"], "cross_listed_codes": [], "course_title": "Attraction and Repulsion: The Magic of Magnets", "course_slug": "3-a08-attraction-and-repulsion-the-magic-of-magnets-fall-2005", "term": "Fall", "year": 2005, "as_taught_in": "Fall 2005", "language": "en", "departments": ["Materials Science and Engineering"], "academic_levels": ["Undergraduate"], "instructors": ["Dr. James Livingston"], "subject_primary": "History", "subject_secondary": ["History of Science and Technology", "Science", "Physics", "Electromagnetism"], "topic_tree": [{"name": "Humanities", "subtopics": [{"name": "History", "specialties": ["History of Science and Technology"]}]}, {"name": "Science", "subtopics": [{"name": "Physics", "specialties": ["Electromagnetism"]}]}], "topic_keywords": [], "pedagogy_tags": [], "assessment_types": [], "course_feature_tags": ["Presentation Assignments", "Written Assignments with Examples"], "description": "This Freshman Advising Seminar surveys the many applications of magnets and magnetism. To the Chinese and Greeks of ancient times, the attractive and repulsive forces between magnets must have seemed magical indeed. Through the ages, miraculous curative powers have been attributed to magnets, and magnets have been used by illusionists to produce “magical” effects. Magnets guided ships in the Age of Exploration and generated the electrical industry in the 19th century. Today they store information and entertainment on disks and tapes, and produce sound in speakers, images on TV screens, rotation in motors, and levitation in high-speed trains. Students visit various MIT projects related to magnets (including superconducting electromagnets) and read about and discuss the history, legends, pseudoscience, science, and technology of types of magnets, including applications in medicine. Sev